\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{RL for multi-turn LLM agents.}
Policy gradient methods---PPO~\cite{schulman2017ppo}, GRPO~\cite{shao2024deepseekmath}, DPO~\cite{rafailov2023dpo}---underpin RLHF~\cite{ouyang2022instructgpt} and have been extended to multi-turn agents~\cite{yao2023react} through step-independent windowing~\cite{ragen2025}. Subsequent work improves algorithms (GiGPO~\cite{feng2025gigpo}), reward design (Self-Guide~\cite{selfguide2026}), or training pipelines (SPEAR~\cite{spear2025}, DigiRL~\cite{digiRL2024}), but all share a common assumption: trajectory decomposition into fixed windows is benign. We show this assumption is false---the windowing introduces a structural credit assignment pathology that persists under default configurations.

\paragraph{Credit assignment in reinforcement learning.}
TD($\lambda$)~\cite{sutton1988td}, GAE~\cite{schulman2016gae}, and TRPO~\cite{schulman2015trpo} interpolate between bootstrapping and Monte Carlo returns; REINFORCE~\cite{williams1992reinforce} uses full episode returns. Reward shaping~\cite{ng1999shaping}, hindsight experience replay~\cite{andrychowicz2017her}, and counterfactual baselines~\cite{foerster2018coma} address sparse or team reward; process reward models~\cite{lightman2024lets,wang2024mathshepherd} provide step-level supervision. In the low-success-rate regime ($p < 0.2$) typical of agent tasks, the classical bias-variance tradeoff breaks down: Proposition~2 shows sign errors under windowing grow as $1{-}p$, dominating any variance reduction that windowing provides.

\paragraph{Credit assignment and sign misattribution evidence.}
Several independent studies corroborate our diagnosis: IRC~\cite{irc2026} reports per-turn advantages frequently disagree in sign with outcome advantages, MC-GRPO~\cite{mcgrpo2025} measures a 5\% sign-flip rate, GRPO-$\lambda$~\cite{grpolambda2025} proves value estimation error grows with position, and OAR~\cite{oar2026} identifies ``reward aliasing'' from coarse credit. We formalize this as a structural property and show it interacts with information loss in a vicious cycle.

\paragraph{Multi-turn credit assignment methods.}
Turn-PPO~\cite{turnppo2026} introduces per-turn value estimation, GAGPO~\cite{gagpo2026} uses a turn-level critic, MT-GRPO~\cite{mtgrpo2025} applies Monte Carlo returns at the turn level, SWEET-RL~\cite{sweetrl2025} combines step-wise rewards with episode-level training, and iStar~\cite{istar2025} learns implicit step-level rewards; a survey~\cite{agenticrl_survey2026} systematizes this landscape. HCAPO~\cite{hcapo2026} applies hindsight credit assignment to ALFWorld agents, validating that sign correctness---not credit granularity per se---is the critical bottleneck. Our work is complementary: we diagnose \textit{why} the default windowing fails, informing when finer-grained credit is safe to use.

\paragraph{GRPO collapse phenomena.}
RAGEN-2~\cite{ragen2_2026} proves advantage collapse when reward variance vanishes, GRPO-Collapse~\cite{grpocollapse2025} identifies a death spiral in Search-R1, Gradient Coupling~\cite{gradientcoupling2025} attributes failure to high input similarity, and RC-GRPO~\cite{rcgrpo2026} and EDGE-GRPO~\cite{edgegrpo2025} address variance collapse through reward conditioning and entropy-driven diversity. Our dual-mechanism diagnosis unifies these: reward variance collapse is a special case of reward saturation (Section~\ref{sec:theory}), and the death spiral follows from the M1--M2 vicious cycle.

